1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,017
Begin with a question. Hold conditions fixed. Change a rule and look again.

2
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:26,007
Higher frequency makes smaller terrain features. The seed, amplitude and three octaves stay fixed.

3
00:00:26,007 --> 00:00:40,008
A finer grid samples the same field more densely. It adds triangles, not a new terrain rule.

4
00:00:40,008 --> 00:00:52,008
Moving the shader threshold changes the snow-colored band. The mesh and its height field stay identical.

5
00:00:52,008 --> 00:01:14,016
Run the same terrain for the same number of steps. Different rain produces different ground and sediment changes.

6
00:01:14,016 --> 00:01:32,016
More moisture changes dry scrub into meadow, forest and wetland where the other conditions allow it.

7
00:01:32,016 --> 00:01:48,008
Sphere, box and spatial noise are different density functions. Marching Cubes extracts each zero surface.

8
00:01:48,008 --> 00:02:00,849
Increase the cutter radius from zero to five. Empty space now passes through solid ground.

9
00:02:00,849 --> 00:02:08,007
Carve first, then add the pillar: operation order restores solid inside the tunnel.

10
00:02:08,007 --> 00:02:20,016
Only chunks intersecting the pocket and its sampling margin need rebuilding. Distant chunks keep their meshes.

11
00:02:20,016 --> 00:02:34,007
Increase volume resolution while holding the density rule fixed. Compare triangles and measured CPU meshing time.

12
00:02:34,007 --> 00:02:38,674
The same pocket touches 8 of 27 chunks. Smaller chunks increase total bookkeeping.

13
00:02:38,674 --> 00:02:46,015
The same pocket touches 12 of 216 chunks. Smaller chunks increase total bookkeeping.

14
00:02:46,015 --> 00:02:54,007
Choose the mesher for the representation. This project extracts smooth density with Marching Cubes; the alternatives are a documented comparison.

15
00:02:54,007 --> 00:03:12,023
Rotate the velocity direction by 90 degrees. At equal speed and time, both sets travel the same distance.

16
00:03:12,023 --> 00:03:32,007
Both grids transport dye. Pressure projection reduces divergence; the readout measures what the picture cannot show.

17
00:03:32,007 --> 00:03:50,007
Parallel rewriting gives 5, 25 and 125 segments. Grammar depth changes structure; it is not elapsed age.

18
00:03:50,007 --> 00:03:58,007
At the same grammar depth, a larger turn angle spreads the branches. Segment count stays fixed.

19
00:03:58,007 --> 00:04:06,023
With reproduction off, the population stays at 104. Growth changes the size of existing plants.

20
00:04:06,023 --> 00:04:15,423
Both stands grow existing trees. Only the right-hand trial is allowed to produce seeds.

21
00:04:15,423 --> 00:04:28,015
With reproduction enabled, new seedlings join the stand. Both trials have the same growth rate and elapsed time.

22
00:04:28,015 --> 00:04:36,015
Crowding lowers health before it necessarily kills a tree. At this short duration, use health as evidence, not a claim of forest collapse.

23
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:45,905
Change tide amplitude, then advance its phase. The same landscape gains and loses dry ground.

24
00:04:45,905 --> 00:04:49,555
High water disconnects the dry route. The trail responds to terrain and sea level.

25
00:04:49,555 --> 00:04:56,023
The tide retreats and the dry connection returns. The forest has been held fixed.

26
00:04:56,023 --> 00:05:08,148
Ground, water, climate and habitat change together on a rotating procedural planet.

27
00:05:08,148 --> 00:05:19,055
The surrounding studies keep their own domains. A shared visual atlas makes the rules legible.

28
00:05:19,055 --> 00:05:28,000
From numbers to landscapes, from fields to life. A world to keep exploring.
